/T (In streaming mode, location signals are matched to roads and combined into live speed estimates. The road network is represented as a graph, with junctions as nodes and road segments as weighted edges, and route finding is a shortest-path search on that graph using algorithms of the Dijkstra and A* family with acceleration techniques for continental-scale networks. Google researchers have also described using graph neural networks to predict the time needed to travel along road segments, which improves arrival-time estimates by learning from historical and live traffic together. This is a direct use of the machine learning methods discussed in Modules 3 and 4.)
/E (In streaming mode, location signals are matched to roads and combined into live speed estimates. The road network is represented as a graph, with junctions as nodes and road segments as weighted edges, and route finding is a shortest-path search on that graph using algorithms of the Dijkstra and A* family with acceleration techniques for continental-scale networks. Google researchers have also described using graph neural networks to predict the time needed to travel along road segments, which improves arrival-time estimates by learning from historical and live traffic together. This is a direct use of the machine learning methods discussed in Modules 3 and 4.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 109 0 R
/T (The output stage turns processed data into what users and other businesses see. These outputs are the map itself, search results for places, route options with a predicted arrival time, turn-by-turn guidance, a colour-coded traffic layer, and incident alerts. The same data is offered to external developers through an application programming interface, so ride-hailing, food-delivery, and logistics applications can use maps and routing inside their own products. The output is also the start of the next cycle: when many users slow down on a road, or take a different route from the one suggested, that behaviour becomes new input data. Table 2.2 summarises the whole flow.)
/E (The output stage turns processed data into what users and other businesses see. These outputs are the map itself, search results for places, route options with a predicted arrival time, turn-by-turn guidance, a colour-coded traffic layer, and incident alerts. The same data is offered to external developers through an application programming interface, so ride-hailing, food-delivery, and logistics applications can use maps and routing inside their own products. The output is also the start of the next cycle: when many users slow down on a road, or take a different route from the one suggested, that behaviour becomes new input data. Table 2.2 summarises the whole flow.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
