653 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 120 0 R
/T (Three observations follow from the numbers. First, gradient flow shrinks as it moves backward. The output delta is -0.0617, while the hidden deltas are -0.0073 and -0.0089, about seven to eight times smaller, and the hidden-layer weight gradients are roughly five to ten times smaller than those of the output layer. The reason is that the sigmoid derivative never exceeds 0.25, so each layer multiplies the signal by a small number. In networks with many layers this repeated shrinking becomes the vanishing- gradient problem, which is why activation functions such as ReLU, residual connections, and gated units like the LSTM were developed.)
/E (Three observations follow from the numbers. First, gradient flow shrinks as it moves backward. The output delta is -0.0617, while the hidden deltas are -0.0073 and -0.0089, about seven to eight times smaller, and the hidden-layer weight gradients are roughly five to ten times smaller than those of the output layer. The reason is that the sigmoid derivative never exceeds 0.25, so each layer multiplies the signal by a small number. In networks with many layers this repeated shrinking becomes the vanishing- gradient problem, which is why activation functions such as ReLU, residual connections, and gated units like the LSTM were developed.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
654 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 120 0 R
/T (Second, the size of each update depends on the learning rate. A step that is too small makes training slow, and one that is too large can overshoot the minimum, so practical training tunes this value. Third, real training is not done on a single example: gradients are averaged over mini-batches of data, optimisers such as Adam adapt the step size, and frameworks like PyTorch and TensorFlow compute all the derivatives automatically. Understanding the manual version, however, explains what those tools are doing and why training can fail. The same algorithm, extended through time, trains recurrent networks, and applied to shared filters it trains convolutional networks.)
/E (Second, the size of each update depends on the learning rate. A step that is too small makes training slow, and one that is too large can overshoot the minimum, so practical training tunes this value. Third, real training is not done on a single example: gradients are averaged over mini-batches of data, optimisers such as Adam adapt the step size, and frameworks like PyTorch and TensorFlow compute all the derivatives automatically. Understanding the manual version, however, explains what those tools are doing and why training can fail. The same algorithm, extended through time, trains recurrent networks, and applied to shared filters it trains convolutional networks.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
