1858 0 obj
<<
/Title (Three observations follow from the numbers. First, gradient flow shrinks as it moves backward. The output delta is -0.0617, while the hidden deltas are -0.0073 and -0.0089, about seven to eight times smaller, and the hidden-layer weight gradients are roughly five to ten times smaller than those of the output layer. The reason is that the sigmoid derivative never exceeds 0.25, so each layer multiplies the signal by a small number. In networks with many layers this repeated shrinking becomes the vanishing- gradient problem, which is why activation functions such as ReLU, residual connections, and gated units like the LSTM were developed.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1837 0 R
/Next 1877 0 R
>>
endobj
1859 0 obj
<<
/Title (In conclusion, backpropagation is the chain rule applied systematically: a forward pass to measure the error, a backward pass to share the blame among the weights, and an update to reduce the error. The following module uses a trained model of a different kind, a conversation system, and examines its logic and limits.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1837 0 R
/Prev 1877 0 R
>>
endobj
