/Title (Second, the size of each update depends on the learning rate. A step that is too small makes training slow, and one that is too large can overshoot the minimum, so practical training tunes this value. Third, real training is not done on a single example: gradients are averaged over mini-batches of data, optimisers such as Adam adapt the step size, and frameworks like PyTorch and TensorFlow compute all the derivatives automatically. Understanding the manual version, however, explains what those tools are doing and why training can fail. The same algorithm, extended through time, trains recurrent networks, and applied to shared filters it trains convolutional networks.)
